--- body ---

## Context

The author wants a causal short paper on JETP acceleration. Country selection is
nonrandom. The independent Astra/Fable proposals and cross-review are in
conception/jetp-causal-design-options-2026-09-14.md; original reports are retained,
including corrections. This ticket makes a design decision after evidence discovery,
not an estimator choice based on apparent effect size. Parent programme: 0725.

## First documentary acceptance check

Challenge a naive JETP-labelled versus old-completed-project comparison with
maturity-based selection and a shared calendar shock. It must fail causal
acceptance despite balanced observed income groups. State what actual assignment
or historical evidence could distinguish these biases from an effect.

## Inputs and candidate designs

0735 / GitHub #1345 audits historical populations, milestone observations and contemporaneous
comparison support. 0736 / GitHub #1346 audits country selection, negotiations, anticipation,
concurrent interventions and any genuine allocation mechanism. Each supplies
locators, contrary evidence and named missing documents, with bounded search logs.

Compare the supported options: exposure of an incumbent pipeline fixed before
intervention; overall pipeline throughput; energy/non-energy triple differences;
country-specific synthetic controls; and a narrower support-component effect if
an actual allocation mechanism exists. A project-support comparison requires an
explicit confounding argument and is distinct from the country partnership effect.
Countries considered but not concluding a JETP are discovery leads, not automatic
controls. No mandatory permutation method, numerical pre-period threshold or
donor-count target is adopted from the initial model reports.

## Design decision

Choose a primary estimand, intervention, population, unit, time zero, outcome and
follow-up horizon only after reviewing the evidence matrices. Country comparability
uses pre-treatment income, financing eligibility and energy-sector conditions;
project comparison uses baseline lender, instrument, technology and maturity.
Across sectors use relevant scale/complexity/procurement characteristics rather
than fictitious technology matches. Define indicators, dates and overlap rules.

Do not condition an incumbent-pipeline effect on approval or survival after JETP.
Earlier comparable landmark populations can provide history; rolling post-onset
risk sets can still contain treatment-induced composition. A throughput estimand
may include new projects, but is not a waiting-time effect. Non-energy spillovers
can make a sector contrast relative rather than total acceleration.

Assess counterfactual assumptions, anticipation, concurrent reforms, spillovers,
measurement change, common support, missingness, cancellation and right/interval
censoring. Good pre-period fit or observed balance supports but does not prove
identification. Few treated countries require a justified country-level uncertainty
procedure; placebo ranks are not exact randomisation p-values without exchangeability.
Distinguish precision/power limitations from failed identification.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Both bounded evidence audits reviewed, including contrary and unavailable evidence
- [x] Candidate-design matrix links each identifying assumption to evidence and unresolved threats
- [x] Causal DEFER justified; no primary estimand/design is authorised
- [x] Comparator definitions, falsification/placebos, sensitivity and uncertainty plan specified
- [x] Protocol proposal records prior outcome inspection and is ready to freeze before 0730 primary estimation
- [x] Author scope decision recorded on DEFER: bounded feasibility screen authorised; no descriptive substitution

No positive or statistically significant effect is required. A narrower valid
causal question is acceptable if named and agreed. If no credible design survives,
return the scope decision to the author. Observatory/data-paper work continues.

## GitHub planning issue

https://github.com/MinhHaDuong/climate-finance-het/issues/1337

## First bounded audit checkpoint, 14 September 2026

0735 and 0736 completed their first 20-unit rounds and received independent
scientific review; report acceptance is tracked in their PRs. The source audits
do not authorize primary estimation. Historical work found two dated World Bank
rosters but no original approval/signature pairs, incomplete landmark coverage
and uncertain stability of roster inclusion rules. Selection work documents
pre-declaration discussions, India's parallel support, and selection partly on
expected energy changes; no usable allocation mechanism was established.

Before a design decision, review the reports under docs/jetp-audits/0735 and0736.
Discriminating next documents are the missing 2015/2021 rosters and original
operation agreements/withdrawals; G7 selection records; dated CIPP screening and
accepted/rejected project lists; and comparator exposure histories. A further
round needs a revised bounded brief. Neither baseline matching nor an absence
of retrieved allocation rules establishes or refutes causality by itself.
No design, exposure baseline or primary outcome is frozen by these audits.

## Broader second pass, 14 September 2026

The author requested coverage beyond World Bank, energy and JETP countries.
The new0735round2report demonstrates ADB/EIB milestone pairs and a downloaded
AFD export:2080financing IDs,1492project IDs,1911award/signature pairs;366loans
and1714grants. See `docs/jetp-audits/0735/round2/report.md` in PR1347.

Prioritize an AFD coverage/retention pilot with ADB/EIB complementary histories,
not a preselected estimator. All AFD rows have signatures, so this export alone
cannot supply approved-but-unsigned risk sets. Missing payment dates, counterparty
consent,2024metadata, multi-country rows and seven reversed award/signature pairs
require investigation. Country location is not JETP attribution or untreated
status. The source data now warrant a broader feasibility exercise; they do not
settle identification. Absence of a randomized allocation rule is not a blanket
rejection of other causal designs.

Next discriminators: an older AFD snapshot and inclusion/removal rules; source
records for date anomalies; stage-specific baseline populations; lender and
sector harmonization; broader-country negotiation/parallel-programme exposure.
Newly archived source bytes are locally DVC-tracked; padme remote replication
remains pending after connection refusal. No primary protocol is frozen.

## Bilateral source reconciliation, third pass

Author-directed AFD/JICA/KfW/USAID/UK audit: see
`docs/jetp-audits/0735/round3/report.md` in PR #1347. AFD current tables contain
3,277 financing IDs; seven-country XML retains 223 of 224 legacy IDs. One
payment-unobserved financing is absent. Payment entries are reporting-period
observations and cannot replace exact legacy first-payment dates. Full-table
reconciliation identifies 19 missing IDs, one missing and one changed signature
proxy. AFD remains the first population/date-semantics pilot.

Complementary routes: 4,036 KfW/BMZ activities with periodic payments; 7,327
FCDO programmes plus 17,955 components, including pipeline and completed cases;
JICA dated annual signing/amendment lists. USAID accounting access is established
but the acquired XML is a historical administrative slice requiring a project-ID
crosswalk. FCDO does not cover every UK financing institution. Do not add these
incommensurate record counts or label non-energy observations untreated.

Next work uses the round-3 memo's explicit first tests: disappearing unpaid-field
records remain loss-to-observation; periodic dates are not exact payment days;
parent/component and signing/amendment entries stay distinct. Build lender- and
stage-specific feasibility tables before selecting identification, with country
conditions and project matching plus the independent selection/exposure audit.
No primary estimator, outcome or exposure baseline is frozen.

## Design checkpoint — 15 September 2026

`docs/jetp-causal-design-decision-0729.md` records the completed documentary
acceptance check, evidence review, candidate-design matrix, causal DEFER,
conditional feasibility protocol and explicit author scope request. It does not
select an estimator, construct a panel or estimate an effect. The outstanding
exit criterion is the author's selection among a bounded pre-outcome feasibility
screen, a clearly descriptive narrowing, or ending the causal-paper path for
now.

## Author decision — 15 September 2026

The author selected the bounded, pre-outcome feasibility screen. This closes
0729's design checkpoint while preserving its causal DEFER: no estimator, panel
or effect estimate is authorised. The next ticket must test the common
country-quarter approval contract and comparison admissibility, then record
GO, NARROW or DEFER before 0730 can begin.
